Article 4 (Duties)
(1) Security management personnel shall perform the following duties under the direction and supervision of the chief officer in order to maintain safety and order in the court buildings and official residence:
1. Confirming the purpose of visits by persons and vehicles seeking access to the court buildings or official residence;
2. Verifying the identity of persons entering the court buildings by resident registration card or other identity documentation, issuing access passes, and checking whether access passes are worn inside the court buildings;
3. Searching persons or vehicles entering the court buildings to determine whether they possess weapons, other dangerous articles, or items that interfere with maintenance of order in the court buildings (hereinafter referred to as "dangerous articles, etc.");
4. Temporarily storing dangerous articles, etc. possessed by persons entering the court buildings, removing them outside the court buildings, or reporting them to the competent police station or requesting cooperation;
5. Restricting access to the court buildings or taking removal measures against any person who:
  (a) possesses dangerous articles, etc.;
  (b) has harmed or is attempting to harm another person's life, body, or property;
  (c) has interfered or is attempting to interfere with the duties of Justices or Constitutional Court officials;
  (d) has disturbed or is attempting to disturb the peace and tranquility of the court buildings;
  (e) repeatedly fails to comply with requests for cooperation or measures by security management personnel such that restriction of access or removal is deemed necessary;
6. Controlling parking within the premises of the court buildings or official residence;
7. Inspecting risk factors through patrols or CCTV monitoring within the premises of the court buildings or official residence;
8. Other duties determined by the Secretary General.
(2) In performing duties under paragraph (1), security management personnel may use the minimum physical force necessary for restraint or use devices such as gas sprayers and portable video recording equipment.
(3) In an emergency requiring protection of a person's life, body, property, or maintenance of order, security management personnel (or, where an on-site manager such as the head of the Unit has been designated, such on-site manager) may take necessary measures, including use of gas sprayers or portable video recording equipment, without awaiting instructions from the chief officer. In such cases, the matter shall be reported to the chief officer without delay.
(4) In performing duties under paragraphs (1) through (3), security management personnel shall take care not to infringe the personality or honor of persons entering the court buildings.
